<BlogPost slug="does-naltrexone-help-with-alcohol-withdrawal" toc={toc}>
  <p>Alcohol withdrawal can be uncomfortable: it's the process of safely removing alcohol from your system after a long period of abusing the drink. Withdrawal affects around half of people who have an alcohol use disorder (AUD) when they stop, or reduce, how much they consume.</p>
  <p>Withdrawal symptoms can be particularly difficult to manage in some cases. For people experiencing severe withdrawal, Medication-Assisted Treatment (MAT) is available to alleviate symptoms. Naltrexone is used in MAT for AUD, but only after you have passed through the detox phase. It does not treat alcohol withdrawal symptoms.</p>
  <p>In this article, I explain clearly what naltrexone is and what role it plays in recovery from alcohol addiction.</p>

  <h2 id="what-is">What Is Naltrexone?</h2>
  <p>Naltrexone is an FDA-approved drug that works as an opioid antagonist. In the US, it's sold under the brand name Revia. It's used to treat AUD and opioid use disorder and is administered in pill form or as an injectable.</p>
  <p>Naltrexone is just one element of treatment for alcohol addiction. It's used in tandem with psychotherapy and holistic activities as part of a comprehensive care plan. Naltrexone shouldn't be taken by anyone under the age of 18 or by anyone experiencing health conditions separate from addiction.</p>
  <p>As a medication for AUD, naltrexone is not that common, but its use has increased over the years. According to one study, treatment of AUD with naltrexone tripled from 2015 to 2018, but still only counted for 1.64% of admissions.</p>

