  <h2 id="how-it-works">How Does Naltrexone Work?</h2>
  <p>In treatment for AUD, you can only begin taking naltrexone after you've finished a <a href="/programs/medical-detox/">detox program</a> and are no longer physically dependent on alcohol.</p>
  <p>Naltrexone works by binding to endorphin receptors in your body, blocking the effects of alcohol. It helps reduce your cravings for alcohol and helps you sustain sobriety as you move forward in treatment. Courses of naltrexone vary, but typically last between three and four months. It is not an addictive substance and doesn't cause its own set of withdrawal symptoms.</p>
  <p>For most people in recovery for alcoholism, naltrexone is given in 50 mg pill form, which is taken daily. Injections are also available and are given every four weeks by a physician. Most people report feeling anti-craving effects as soon as 24 hours after the first dose of naltrexone.</p>

  <h2 id="side-effects">Does Naltrexone Have Side Effects?</h2>
  <p>Yes, there are possible side effects with naltrexone, which include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Nausea</li>
    <li>Drowsiness</li>
    <li>Reduced appetite</li>
    <li>Headache</li>
    <li>Vomiting</li>
    <li>Muscle aches</li>
    <li>Problems sleeping</li>
    <li>Toothache</li>
  </ul>
  <p>More severe side effects are rare and include liver damage, pneumonia, and depressed mood. Naltrexone does not cause a severe physical reaction when combined with alcohol, but that does not make it a safe combination. Drinking while on naltrexone can strain the liver and may mask how intoxicated you actually are, leading you to drink more than intended.</p>

  <h2 id="effectiveness">How Effective Is Naltrexone for Alcohol Addiction?</h2>
  <p>Multiple studies have confirmed the effectiveness of naltrexone in AUD treatment. One study found that 72% of patients reported significantly less craving for alcohol. Another noted naltrexone reduced the risk of relapse by nearly 40%.</p>
  <p>As with any medication, there are genetic and environmental factors that impact how effective naltrexone is.</p>
  <p><b>Genetics.</b> Your genetics not only play a role in how likely you are to develop an AUD, but also in the effectiveness of naltrexone. People who have family members who have successfully recovered from AUD may respond better to the drug.</p>
  <p><b>Relationship with alcohol.</b> Some studies suggest that naltrexone is more effective for people who consume alcohol for its rewarding effects rather than to avoid withdrawal symptoms.</p>
  <p><b>Dose.</b> Naltrexone is generally well tolerated, but it's essential that your clinician monitors your dose and frequency of the drug to help alleviate side effects and to ensure that the medication has the desired outcome.</p>

  <h2 id="mat">What's the Difference Between MAT and Naltrexone?</h2>
  <p><a href="/programs/medication-assisted-treatment/">Medication-Assisted Treatment (MAT)</a> is when FDA-approved medications are used to reduce withdrawal symptoms and cravings, as well as to lower the risk of relapse. Naltrexone is one of the medications used for AUD, often alongside acamprosate and disulfiram.</p>
  <p>MAT can be used in detox or during following treatment programs such as <a href="/programs/residential-treatment/">residential care</a> and outpatient programs. Naltrexone, however, is only used after you have finished detox. It therefore does not support during alcohol withdrawal — what it does is reduce cravings and keep them at bay once you have removed alcohol from your system and are stable.</p>
  <p>As part of integrated treatment, naltrexone can help you abstain from alcohol, build a healthier relationship with the drink, and continue in recovery with greater confidence.</p>

